{
  "programId": "com.digitalarsenal.flows.geonames-ingest",
  "name": "GeoNames Gazetteer Ingest Flow",
  "version": "0.2.0",
  "engineLinkage": "bridge",
  "description": "Scheduled ingest of a gazetteer into $GNP, on the typed contracts (1.0): every frame between nodes is a FlatBuffer, no JSON and no base64. A 24 h host-cron TimerTick enters at geonames-ingest:mark_query, which builds the $FSO that reads the newest $IRM resume marks (SELECT _data FROM IRM ORDER BY _rowid DESC LIMIT ?); hostcap/flatsql-query answers $FSB chunks to geonames-ingest:ingest_plan, which picks this dataset's mark by JOB_ID and decides between three outcomes and only three. FIRST TICK (no mark) = the SEED: one ranged HttpRequest for cities15000.zip plus three more for the division and country files, because $GNP carries a RESOLVED admin and country NAME beside every code and those names live in separate files. EVERY LATER TICK = the DELTA for that tick's own UTC day: modifications-YYYY-MM-DD.txt and deletes-YYYY-MM-DD.txt, verified live 2026-08-15 to be full 19-column geoname rows and three-column tombstone rows respectively - so the delta upserts through the SAME decoder as the seed. A TICK FOR A DAY ALREADY ON THE MARK emits nothing at all: the mark IS the same-data ledger, carrying the upstream ETag that also rides out as if-none-match, so an unchanged file costs one conditional request and comes back 304, which geonames-source:inflate ends the run on with an Unchanged FlowNotice. Every fetch carries its JOB in HttpRequest.CONTEXT - one size-prefixed $GNP header record naming the dataset contract (id prefix, member, dataset id/name/URL/epoch, source URL and file, licence, attribution) - echoed by hostcap/http-request and read by geonames-source and ingest_meta. The seed archive is inflated IN-GUEST (vendored miniz; there is no host unzip capability and none may be added) into a text HttpResponse; geonames-source:parse_places joins the three lookup HttpResponses and decodes the rows, and the chunk is bounded by the Go server host's 4 MiB response-body cap, which ingest_meta re-checks against Content-Range's denominator rather than trusting. geonames-ingest:ingest_meta authors the IngestRequest (INGQ: GNP.fbs, batch <dataset>@<day>, RECONCILE None because a daily modifications file is an upsert of a few thousand rows and not the gazetteer's set, PROVENANCE with the ETag / Last-Modified / record count) before hostcap/storage-ingest persists the $GNP stream on gnp_records through the policy-mediated storage.ingest_with_source op. geonames-ingest:publish_request then advances the mark FROM THE STORAGE RESULT - an $IRM, never at dispatch and never for a refused or stored-nothing batch, so a crash between fetch and store re-fetches one day instead of skipping it invisibly - and POSTs the epoch announcement (body: the IngestResult naming the batch) only when geonames_publish_url is configured; DATASET_EPOCH and DATASET_CID already ride in $GNP.SOURCE, so no new record type is minted. The $IRM mark, the IngestResult, the publication response and the FlowNotices (inflate report, parse summary, tombstone list) leave on egress: no host capability removes a stored row, and inventing one would be a new host capability this lane may not add; persisting the mark is hostcap/storage-write's job and is not wired here (the flow declares no storage_write grant). The host owns no schedule, no dataset list, no licence and no record semantics: it contributes timers, HTTP egress, guarded persistence and the FlatSQL rows that hold the marks.",
